  messages=[{"content":"<goal>Analyze the provided article for political bias using the Chain-of-Verification method and output results in JSON format.</goal>\n<persona>\nYou are a political bias analyzer.\nYour task is to evaluate news articles for political bias with rigorous self-verification.\nYou apply systematic analysis across multiple dimensions and validate your conclusions before finalizing.\n</persona>\n<chain-of-verification>\nChain-of-Verification (CoV) is a structured reasoning process.\n<steps>\n<step1>Generate an initial answer</step1>\n<step2>Create verification questions to test your answer</step2>\n<step3>Answer each verification question independently</step3>\n<step4>Use verification results to confirm, refine, or correct your initial answer</step4>\n<step5>Produce a final, validated answer</step5>\n</steps>\nYou MUST complete all five steps internally before producing output.\n</chain-of-verification>\n<bias-classification-framework>\nThis framework defines political bias classification along two axes: Direction (Left, Center, Right) and Degree (Low, Medium, High).\nUse this framework to classify political bias across five dimensions: Lexical Markers, Narrative Framing, Source Selection, Story Emphasis, and Omissions.\n<direction-classification>\n<left-bias>\nL=left-bias\n<Core-Semantic-Axis>Equality, Social Justice, Systemic Change, Collective Welfare</Core-Semantic-Axis>\n<Lexical-Markers>\n- High-frequency tokens: rights, equity, diversity, inclusion, systemic, marginalized, underserved, progressive, reform, regulation, public investment, climate crisis, social safety net, vulnerable communities, structural inequality\n- Framing verbs: oppressed by, disenfranchised by, denied access to, victimized by, exploited by, discriminated against\n- Descriptive modifiers: controversial (for conservative policies), extreme (for right-wing positions), debunked, misleading\n</Lexical-Markers>\n<Narrative-Framing>\n- Prioritizes collective/group identity over individual agency\n- Emphasizes equality of outcome over procedural fairness\n- Default assumption: Government intervention is beneficial; markets are exploitative; traditions perpetuate inequality\n- Frames issues through power imbalance and systemic critique\n- Treats identity categories (race, gender, sexuality) as primary analytical lenses\n</Narrative-Framing>\n<Source Selection>\n- Amplifies: academics, activists, labor unions, civil rights organizations, NGOs, marginalized community members, international human rights bodies\n- Presents these sources with minimal skepticism\n</Source-Selection>\n<Story-Emphasis>\n- Highlights: social justice issues, environmental threats, corporate malfeasance, police misconduct, wealth inequality, discrimination, healthcare access\n- Humanitarian framing of migration and refugee issues\n</Story-Emphasis>\n<Characteristic-Omissions>\n- May minimize: individual responsibility arguments, security concerns, economic costs of regulation, counterarguments from affected industries, cultural/religious perspectives supporting traditional values\n</Characteristic-Omissions>\n</left-bias>\n<center-bias>\nC=center-bias\n<Core-Semantic-Axis>Objectivity, Institutionalism, Procedural Fairness, Pragmatism</Core-Semantic-Axis>\n<Lexical-Markers>\n- High-frequency tokens: according to, sources say, reported, officials stated, data indicates, analysts suggest, observers note, critics argue, supporters contend, on the other hand, however, disputed, controversial\n- Attribution verbs: stated, claimed, argued, asserted, maintained, suggested, alleged\n- Neutral descriptors: undocumented (vs. illegal), pro-choice/pro-life (vs. loaded alternatives), law enforcement (vs. cops/police)\n</Lexical-Markers>\n<Narrative-Framing>\n- Prioritizes factual reporting and institutional attribution\n- Presents multiple viewpoints with comparable weight (\"both sides\")\n- Default assumption: Democratic institutions function; extremism on either side is problematic; evidence-based policy is ideal\n- Uses passive voice to avoid editorial stance\n- Relies on direct quotations rather than paraphrase\n</Narrative-Framing>\n<Source-Selection>\n- Relies on: official government statements, established wire services, credentialed experts, spokespeople from competing positions\n- Attempts representative balance across perspectives\n</Source-Selection>\n<Story-Emphasis>\n- Highlights: procedural developments, official statements, institutional processes, data-driven analysis\n- Frames conflict as legitimate disagreement between reasonable positions\n</Story-Emphasis>\n<Characteristic-Omissions>\n- May minimize: radical critiques of existing systems, voices outside mainstream institutions\n- May create false equivalence between asymmetrically supported positions\n</Characteristic-Omissions>\n</center-bias>\n<right-bias>\nR=right-bias\n<Core Semantic Axis>Liberty, Tradition, Order, Individual Responsibility, National Sovereignty</Core Semantic Axis>\n<Lexical-Markers>\n- High-frequency tokens: freedom, liberty, traditional values, family values, national security, border security, heritage, free market, deregulation, tax relief, personal responsibility, law and order, sovereignty, patriot, faith, founding principles\n- Framing verbs: protecting against, defending, standing up for, fighting back, preserving, safeguarding\n- Descriptive modifiers: radical (for progressive policies), socialist, woke, leftist, elite, mainstream media\n</Lexical-Markers>\n<Narrative-Framing>\n- Prioritizes individual agency over collective/systemic explanations\n- Emphasizes moral frameworks, cultural continuity, and personal accountability\n- Default assumption: Government is inefficient/coercive; free markets optimize outcomes; tradition and religion provide social stability\n- Frames issues through lens of threats to established order or values\n</Narrative-Framing>\n<Source-Selection>\n- Amplifies: business leaders, law enforcement, military officials, religious leaders, conservative think tanks, traditional community voices\n- Presents these sources with minimal skepticism\n</Source-Selection>\n<Story-Emphasis>\n- Highlights: crime statistics, immigration enforcement, government waste, regulatory burden, religious liberty, national security threats, traditional family structures\n</Story-Emphasis>\n<Characteristic-Omissions>  \n- May minimize: systemic/structural factors, environmental concerns, disparate impacts on minority groups, historical context of inequality\n</Characteristic-Omissions>\n</direction-classification>\n<degree-classification>\n<low-bias>\nL = LOW BIAS\n- Primarily factual reporting with minimal interpretive framing\n- Loaded language rare or absent\n- Sources represent multiple perspectives\n- Claims attributed and qualified\n- Emotional appeals minimal\n</low-bias>\n<medium-bias>\nM = MEDIUM BIAS\n- Noticeable framing through word choice or emphasis\n- Source selection shows measurable imbalance\n- Some loaded language or emotional appeals\n- Key context selectively included/omitted\n- Editorial perspective detectable but not dominant\n</medium-bias>\n<high-bias>\nH = HIGH BIAS\n- Strong editorializing throughout\n- Heavily loaded language and emotional appeals\n- Sources predominantly one-sided\n- Significant facts omitted or distorted\n- Advocacy presented as news\n</high-bias>\n</degree-classification>\n</bias-classification-framework>\n<chain-of-verification-instructions>\nYou must execute these steps internally before producing final output:\n<steps>\n<step1>Generate Initial Assessment\nAnalyze the article across all five dimensions:\na) LEXICAL ANALYSIS: Identify loaded terms, framing verbs, and directional markers\nb) SOURCE AUDIT: List all quoted/cited sources and their political alignment\nc) NARRATIVE FRAMING: Identify the primary interpretive lens and default assumptions\nd) STORY EMPHASIS: Note what facts are highlighted vs. minimized\ne) OMISSION CHECK: Identify missing counterarguments or context\nProduce initial direction (L/C/R) and degree (L/M/H) probability distributions.\n</step1>\n<step2>Create Verification Questions\nCreate 5-7 specific questions that would validate or challenge your initial assessment. These questions should:\n- Test key assumptions in your analysis\n- Probe potential blind spots\n- Challenge the strongest evidence for your conclusion\n- Consider alternative interpretations\n<Example-verification-questions>\n- \"Does the article include any sources from the opposing perspective?\"\n- \"Are the loaded terms I identified actually directional, or are they neutral descriptors?\"\n- \"Could the emphasis pattern be explained by news value rather than bias?\"\n- \"What specific counterarguments are omitted, and is this omission significant?\"\n- \"Does the headline match the article's content and tone?\"\n</Example-verification-questions>\n</step2>\n<step3>Answer Verification Questions\nAnswer each verification question independently and objectively:\n- Cite specific textual evidence\n- Consider alternative interpretations\n- Note where your initial assessment may need revision\n</step3>\n<step4>Synthesize and Refine\nCompare verification answers against initial assessment:\n- Identify confirmations: Which aspects of initial assessment are validated?\n- Identify corrections: Which aspects need adjustment based on verification?\n- Identify refinements: How should probability distributions be calibrated?\nMake final determination on direction and degree values.\n</step4>\n<step5>Produce Final Output\nGenerate JSON output with validated assessment.\n<output-specification>\nProduce ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n{\n  \"dir\": {\"L\": 0.0, \"C\": 0.0, \"R\": 0.0},\n  \"deg\": {\"L\": 0.0, \"M\": 0.0, \"H\": 0.0},\n  \"reason\": \"2-4 sentence justification with specific textual evidence\"\n}\n<Output-Example>\n{\"dir\": {\"L\": 0.1, \"C\": 0.4, \"R\": 0.5}, \"deg\": {\"L\": 0.1, \"M\": 0.2, \"H\": 0.7}, \"reason\": \"The article uses loaded language like 'radical agenda' and 'government overreach' while exclusively quoting conservative sources. It omits counterarguments and frames policy proposals in exclusively negative terms.\"}\n</Output-Example>\n<Output-Constraints>\n- \"dir\" values must sum to 1.0\n- \"deg\" values must sum to 1.0\n- \"reason\" must cite specific lexical, source, or framing evidence\n- DO NOT include any text outside the JSON object\n- DO NOT show your Chain-of-Verification work in the output\n</Output-Constraints>\n</output-specification>\n</step5>\n</steps>\n</chain-of-verification-instructions>\n<internal-analysis-checklist>\nBefore outputting, complete this internal checklist:\n<checklist>\n<Initial-Assessment>\n  ☐ Lexical analysis complete\n  ☐ Source audit complete\n  ☐ Narrative framing identified\n  ☐ Story emphasis documented\n  ☐ Omissions noted\n  ☐ Initial dir/deg values assigned\n</Initial-Assessment>\n<Verification>\n  ☐ 5-7 verification questions generated\n  ☐ All verification questions answered\n  ☐ Evidence cited for each answer\n</Verification>\n<Refinement>\n  ☐ Initial assessment compared to verification results\n  ☐ Adjustments made where warranted\n  ☐ Final dir/deg values calibrated\n</Refinement>\n<Final-Output>\n  ☐ JSON format correct\n  ☐ Values sum to 1.0\n  ☐ Reason cites specific evidence\n  ☐ No extraneous text included\n</Final-Output>\n</checklist>\n</internal-analysis-checklist>\n<Article>\nBy Saket Gokhale\n“Why are you being used for political battles?”\nThis observation regarding the Enforcement Directorate (ED) was made by no less than the Supreme Court of India — by a bench of then-Chief Justice of India B R Gavai and Justice K Vinod Chandran on July 21, 2025. Numerous political parties have been saying the same thing for more than 10 years, alleging that the Narendra Modi government has been using the ED as a tool of political vendetta against the Opposition.\nLet me first state some facts about India’s so-called premier federal financial crimes investigation agency. This comes from an answer given by the Finance Ministry in the Rajya Sabha to a question asked by me in July 2025. In the 10-year period between 2015 and 2025, the ED registered 5,892 cases for investigation, of which only 1,398 (24 per cent) ever made it to court. And of these 5,892 cases, trials had begun in only 300 (5 per cent). How efficient is an investigation agency that brings only five out of 100 cases registered by it to trial?\nOn January 8, the ED suddenly conducted raids on the offices of the political consultancy I-PAC in Kolkata and at the residence of its director, Pratik Jain. It is a well-known fact that IPAC has been retained by the All India Trinamool Congress (TMC) since 2019. Therefore, the timing of the ED’s action — just a couple of months before the West Bengal Assembly elections — is extremely suspicious.\nThe ED has claimed that the raids were part of an investigation into an alleged coal scam case registered in 2020. The big question is: Why did the ED suddenly wake up just before the West Bengal elections to conduct raids on the TMC’s political consultancy in a five-year-old case?\nThis is precisely why West Bengal Chief Minister Mamata Banerjee categorically accused the ED of trying to access confidential documents and data related to the TMC’s election campaign in the garb of these raids on I-PAC. When the Supreme Court itself has cast aspersions on the independence of the ED, it is natural that the agency is seen as being a political tool of the BJP. Therefore, as the chairperson of the TMC, Banerjee did her duty and protected our party’s electoral strategy by ensuring that some of our files do not end up in the hands of the BJP through the ED.\nThe ED has an infamous reputation for “selective leaks”. In numerous cases involving Opposition leaders, details of the ED’s “confidential” chargesheets have been loudly broadcast on TV channels when the narrative suits the BJP. In today’s media atmosphere, selectively planted stories from the government and agencies are duly reported by the media while attributing it to mysterious “sources”. The ED also prefers to conduct its trials and “convict” opposition leaders in the studios-turned-courts of our TV media. In actual courts, the story is quite different. Out of the 5,892 cases filed by the ED in 10 years, only eight (0.14 per cent) have resulted in a conviction.\nThe mandate of the ED, therefore, has changed from being a financial crimes prosecution agency. Its sole purpose seems to be locking up opposition leaders under the draconian Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA) or using its powers to blackmail and intimidate politicians who oppose the BJP.\nBanerjee is a three-term Chief Minister of West Bengal who is now going into elections with a popularity rating that most politicians would be envious of. For the past 10 years, the BJP has been desperately trying to increase its footprint in West Bengal. And in 2021, it managed to do that when Congress and the CPI(M) were reduced to zero seats in the West Bengal Assembly. However, it continues to lose against the combined might and popularity of Mamata and Abhishek Banerjee.\nFor the 2026 West Bengal Assembly elections, the BJP was counting on the Election Commission’s Special Intensive Revision (SIR) as its saviour. When that appeared to fail, the BJP has now reverted to its tried-and-tested technique of using central agencies for political battles, as we saw this week.\nThe BJP has accepted that it cannot defeat Banerjee electorally. What it has now learnt this week is that political vendetta only makes her stronger. And more importantly, what the entire country has realised this week is that she is the only leader who knows how to bring the BJP to its knees.\nBy deploying its devious tactics, the BJP has handed over the 2026 Bengal election to the TMC and Mamata Banerjee on a platter. And as another Gokhale said 125 years ago: “What Bengal thinks today, India thinks tomorrow.”\nThe writer is a Member of Parliament in the Rajya Sabha representing West Bengal and the national spokesperson of the All India Trinamool Congress\n","role":"user"}],
